= Climate change =

== Overview ==
'''Climate change''' is a persistent change in the statistical characteristics of climate, including the distribution of temperature, precipitation and other variables over decades or longer. It can have natural causes and human causes. In contemporary scientific assessment, the term often refers specifically to the rapid human-driven warming and associated changes observed since industrialization.

== Climate versus weather ==
Weather describes conditions and events on short timescales, whereas climate concerns distributions and patterns over longer periods. A cold day or one storm does not establish or refute a long-term climate trend. Researchers use many independent observations, reconstructed records and physical models to evaluate change.

== Physical causes and attribution ==
Greenhouse gases absorb and emit thermal infrared radiation and alter Earth's energy balance. Human activities have increased atmospheric concentrations of greenhouse gases, primarily through fossil-fuel use and land-use change. IPCC AR6 (2023) concludes that human activities, principally greenhouse-gas emissions, have unequivocally caused global warming. It reports global surface temperature in 2011–2020 at about 1.1 °C above 1850–1900; this is a dated assessment interval, not a current-year estimate.

Natural variability, solar and volcanic influences, land-use change and aerosols also affect climate. Attribution research compares expected patterns and magnitudes from multiple forcings against observations and uncertainty. Recognizing these influences does not make the observed warming equally attributable to all causes.

== Connections to carbon and the ocean ==
[[Related to::Carbon cycle|The carbon cycle]] describes exchanges of carbon among atmosphere, land, ocean and geological reservoirs. When added anthropogenic carbon dioxide remains in the atmosphere, it increases radiative forcing. Ocean uptake of carbon dioxide changes seawater carbonate chemistry and contributes to [[Related to::Ocean acidification|ocean acidification]], a related chemical process rather than the same quantity as atmospheric warming.

== Impacts and uncertainties ==
Changes affect heat extremes, precipitation patterns, glaciers, sea level and ecosystems, with regional differences and multiple mechanisms. Attribution and projected change require appropriate baseline periods, geographic scope, scenarios and confidence language. Scientific uncertainty about specific regional outcomes does not erase the established broad-scale human contribution to observed warming.

== Evidence standards ==
The IPCC synthesizes many published studies and labels confidence and likelihood. A synthesis report is authoritative evidence assessment, not a new single experiment. The reported figures and descriptions should be connected to the edition and observation period; neither global averages nor projections should be substituted for a location-specific forecast.

== Measurement and computational inference ==
For a computation-centered treatment, the first question is not whether Earth is itself a computer, but '''what observations and inference procedures allow a specified climatic quantity to be estimated'''. A time series of surface temperature, precipitation, ocean heat or another variable requires defined sensors or source datasets, location, temporal coverage, calibration, units, aggregation rules and uncertainty. The baseline period is part of the claim: comparing two temperatures without specifying where and when they were measured is not a reproducible estimate of change.

A climate model is a '''human-constructed computational representation''' of physical processes under specified assumptions and initial/boundary conditions. It can combine observations, physical constraints and numerical methods to produce estimates or scenarios. Simulated outputs, reconstructed historical values and directly observed measurements have different provenance; an inference from data is not itself a sensor reading. A forecast or scenario must specify the model and conditions rather than silently becoming an observation.

These methods concern measurement, data processing, model verification and uncertainty. They '''do not imply that the planet inherently computes''' in the same mechanistic sense as a digital circuit or a specific biological signaling system. See [[Measurement]], [[Scientific model]], [[Uncertainty]] and [[BITwiki:Computational scope]].

